{title2=Algebra and Geometry}

= i
{parent=8C}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**Yes.** Suppose $\alpha_1a_1+\alpha_2a_2+\alpha_3a_3=0$. Taking its <dot product> with $a_2\times a_3$ eliminates the last two terms and gives
$$
\alpha_1\,a_1\cdot(a_2\times a_3)=0.
$$
The nonzero <scalar triple product> forces $\alpha_1=0$. Cyclically using $a_3\times a_1$ and $a_1\times a_2$ forces $\alpha_2=\alpha_3=0$ as well. This is the definition of <linear independence>. Equivalently the three-column <matrix> has nonzero <determinant>.

= ii
{parent=8C}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**No.** For every $n\geq2$, take
$$
a_j=e_j\quad(1\leq j\leq n-1),\qquad
 a_n=e_1+\cdots+e_{n-1}
$$
in $\mathbb R^n$. These form a <minimally dependent vector family>: the full family is dependent because $a_n-\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}a_j=0$.

If the omitted <vector> is $a_n$, the remaining coordinate <vectors> are independent. If the omitted <vector> is $e_k$, a relation among the retained <vectors> has an $e_k$ coordinate coming only from $a_n$, so its coefficient must be zero; the coefficients of all retained coordinate <vectors> must then be zero. Thus every $(n-1)$-element subfamily is <linearly independent>, as required for a counterexample. When $n=1$, the empty subfamily is independent while the one-vector family consisting of the <zero vector> is not, so the answer remains negative.

= iii
{parent=8C}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A direct construction, valid for every $0\leq t<1$, is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
a_1&=(1,0,0),\\
a_2&=(t,\sqrt{1-t^2},0),\\
a_3&=\left(t,\ t\sqrt{\frac{1-t}{1+t}},\
\sqrt{\frac{(1-t)(1+2t)}{1+t}}\right).
\end{aligned}}
$$
The first coordinates give $a_1\cdot a_2=a_1\cdot a_3=t$, and
$$
a_2\cdot a_3=t^2+t(1-t)=t.
$$
The first two norms are one. For the third,
$$
|a_3|^2=t^2+\frac{t^2(1-t)+(1-t)(1+2t)}{1+t}=1.
$$
Thus the required <Gram matrix> is $(1-t)I+t\mathbf1\mathbf1^T$. The column <matrix> is upper triangular and has <determinant>
$$
\boxed{\det(a_1\ a_2\ a_3)=(1-t)\sqrt{1+2t}>0,}
$$
so these <vectors> are <linearly independent>. This is an explicit instance of <unit vectors with a common inner product>; at $t=0$ it reduces to the standard <orthonormal basis>.

