= Solution
{parent=i}

<Rolle's theorem> states that if a real <function> $h$ is continuous on $[a,b]$, differentiable on $(a,b)$, with $a<b$ and $h(a)=h(b)$, then some $c\in(a,b)$ satisfies $h'(c)=0$.

= ii
{parent=9D}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the two-function assertion, define
$$
H(x)=[f(b)-f(a)][g(x)-g(a)]
-[g(b)-g(a)][f(x)-f(a)].
$$
This <function> has the required continuity and differentiability and satisfies $H(a)=H(b)=0$. <Rolle's theorem> therefore gives $c$ with
$$
\boxed{[f(b)-f(a)]g'(c)=[g(b)-g(a)]f'(c).}
$$
This proves the <Cauchy mean value theorem> even when either endpoint increment vanishes; no division was used.

= iii
{parent=9D}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Now impose the stated zero endpoint values and nonvanishing assumptions near $a$. For each $x\in(a,a+\delta)$, applying the proved identity on $[a,x]$ gives some $c_x\in(a,x)$ such that
$$
\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=\frac{f'(c_x)}{g'(c_x)}.
$$
As $x\downarrow a$, every possible $c_x$ also tends to $a$. Explicitly, for any $\varepsilon>0$, choose a right neighborhood of $a$ where $|f'/g'-L|<\varepsilon$. If $x$ lies in it, so does $c_x$, and therefore $|f(x)/g(x)-L|<\varepsilon$. This proves the endpoint zero-over-zero form of <L'Hopital's rule>:
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to a+}f(x)/g(x)=L.}
$$
No continuity of either <derivative> is required, only their ratio limit and the given nonvanishing conditions.

= iv
{parent=9D}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For the final limit, near zero $\cos x>0$, so the real logarithm is well defined. First apply the proved rule to $\tan x/x$ on the right: its <derivative> ratio is $\sec^2x\to1$. Next apply it to $\log(\cos x)/x^2$; the <derivative> ratio is
$$
\frac{-\tan x}{2x}\longrightarrow-\frac12.
$$
The original quotient is even, so the left limit equals the right limit. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\log\cos x}{x^2}=-\frac12.}
$$

= 10E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=10E}

Use <integration by parts> on $\sin^{n-1}x\sin x$, for $n\geq2$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
I_n
&=[-\sin^{n-1}x\cos x]_0^{\pi/2}
+(n-1)\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin^{n-2}x\cos^2x\,dx\\
&=(n-1)(I_{n-2}-I_n).
\end{aligned}
$$
Both boundary terms are zero. Therefore the <Wallis integrals> satisfy
$$
\boxed{nI_n=(n-1)I_{n-2}.}
$$
Starting with $I_0=\pi/2$ and $I_1=1$, repeated application gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
I_{2n}&=\frac{1\cdot3\cdots(2n-1)}{2\cdot4\cdots2n}\frac\pi2
=\frac{(2n)!}{2^{2n}(n!)^2}\frac\pi2,\\
I_{2n+1}&=\frac{2\cdot4\cdots2n}{3\cdot5\cdots(2n+1)}
=\frac{2^{2n}(n!)^2}{(2n+1)!}.
\end{aligned}
$$
These formulas also hold at $n=0$ by empty products.

For $0<x<\pi/2$, $0<\sin x<1$, so $0<\sin^n x<\sin^{n-1}x$ when $n\geq1$. Integrating the strict inequalities proves $0<I_n<I_{n-1}$. In particular $I_{2n+1}/I_{2n}<1$. For $n\geq1$, the recurrence and $I_{2n-1}>I_{2n}$ give
$$
I_{2n+1}=\frac{2n}{2n+1}I_{2n-1}
>\frac{2n}{2n+1}I_{2n}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\frac{2n}{2n+1}<\frac{I_{2n+1}}{I_{2n}}<1.}
$$
At $n=0$ the same displayed bounds are $0<2/\pi<1$, also true directly from $I_1<I_0$.

The <squeeze theorem> now gives $I_{2n+1}/I_{2n}\to1$. Substituting the evaluated integrals shows
$$
\frac{I_{2n+1}}{I_{2n}}
=\frac1\pi\frac{2^{4n+1}(n!)^4}{(2n+1)((2n)!)^2},
$$
and hence
$$
\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{2^{4n+1}(n!)^4}{(2n+1)((2n)!)^2}=\pi.}
$$
This is twice the finite-product form of the <Wallis product>; the proof used only the integral recurrence and strict comparison of its integrands.

= 11F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=11F}

For the <additive function>, setting both arguments to zero gives $f(0)=0$, and then $f(-x)=-f(x)$. Continuity at the given point $x_0$ implies
$$
f(h)=f(x_0+h)-f(x_0)\longrightarrow0\qquad(h\to0).
$$
For any real $x$, $f(x+h)-f(x)=f(h)$, so $f$ is continuous at every point. This proves the continuity part of <additive function continuous at one point is linear>.

Let $c=f(1)$. Repeated addition and negation give $f(m)=mc$ for every integer $m$. For integers $p$ and positive $q$,
$$
qf(p/q)=f(p)=pc,
$$
so $f(r)=cr$ for every <rational number> $r$. Given any real $x$, choose rational $r_n\to x$. Continuity and <density of the rational numbers> give
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\lim_n f(r_n)=\lim_n cr_n=cx.}
$$
Conversely each real multiple of $x$ satisfies the <Cauchy functional equation>.

For the multiplicative equation, assume first that $g$ is not identically zero and choose $x_1$ with $g(x_1)\ne0$. Then $g(x_1)=g(x_1)g(0)$ forces $g(0)=1$. Next
$$
g(x)g(-x)=1
$$
makes every value nonzero, and
$$
g(x)=g(x/2)^2>0
$$
makes every value positive. Thus $h(x)=\log g(x)$ is a continuous real <additive function>, since $h(x+y)=h(x)+h(y)$. The proved classification gives $h(x)=kx$ for a real constant $k$. This solves the <continuous exponential functional equation>:
$$
\boxed{g(x)\equiv0\quad\text{or}\quad g(x)=e^{kx}\ (k\in\mathbb R).}
$$
Both types satisfy the equation and continuity, and every nonzero solution is everywhere strictly positive.

= 12F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis}

= i
{parent=12F}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Rearranging the assumed positive-term ratio inequality gives
$$
\frac{a_{n+1}}{b_{n+1}}\leq\frac{a_n}{b_n}.
$$
Induction therefore yields $a_n/b_n\leq a_1/b_1$ for every $n\geq1$. In particular
$$
0\leq\sum_{n=1}^N a_n
\leq\frac{a_1}{b_1}\sum_{n=1}^N b_n
\leq\frac{a_1}{b_1}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}b_n.
$$
The positive <partial sums> of the first <series> are nondecreasing and bounded above, so the <bounded monotone sequence theorem> proves that they converge. This is the <ratio comparison test>, and establishes **convergence of $\sum a_n$**.

= ii
{parent=12F}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Extend the <sequence> by $c_0=1$. Cancelling factorials in consecutive <normalized central binomial coefficients> gives
$$
\frac{c_n}{c_{n-1}}=\frac{(2n)(2n-1)}{4n^2}
=1-\frac1{2n},\qquad
c_n=\prod_{j=1}^n\left(1-\frac1{2j}\right).
$$
The given logarithmic inequality therefore implies
$$
\log c_n\leq-\frac12\sum_{j=1}^n\frac1j.
$$
On $[j,j+1]$, $1/t\leq1/j$, so summing its integrals gives $\sum_{j=1}^n1/j\geq\log(n+1)$. Exponentiating yields the explicit bound
$$
\boxed{0<c_n\leq(n+1)^{-1/2}\longrightarrow0.}
$$
Also every consecutive ratio is less than one, so $(c_n)$ is strictly decreasing, a fact needed for the alternating endpoint.

= iii
{parent=12F}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At $x=1/4$ the terms are $c_n$. Their decay to zero alone is insufficient for convergence. In fact
$$
\frac{c_{n+1}}{c_n}=\frac{2n+1}{2n+2}
\geq\frac{n+1}{n+2},
$$
because the difference has numerator $n\geq0$. Since $c_1=1/2$, induction gives $c_n\geq1/(n+1)$. The comparison <harmonic series> diverges, so
$$
\boxed{x=1/4:\ \text{the series diverges to }+\infty.}
$$

At $x=-1/4$ the terms are $(-1)^nc_n$. Part (ii) gives positive decreasing magnitudes tending to zero, so the <alternating series test> applies. To see the convergence directly, let $S_N=\sum_{n=1}^N(-1)^nc_n$. Then
$$
S_{2k+2}-S_{2k}=-c_{2k+1}+c_{2k+2}<0,
$$
while $S_{2k}\geq-c_1$, obtained by grouping $S_{2k}=-c_1+(c_2-c_3)+\cdots+(c_{2k-2}-c_{2k-1})+c_{2k}$. Thus the even <partial sums> have a finite limit. The odd <partial sums> differ from them by $-c_{2k+1}\to0$, so the whole <sequence> of <partial sums> has the same limit. Absolute convergence would require convergence of $\sum c_n$, already disproved. Hence
$$
\boxed{x=-1/4:\ \text{the series converges conditionally}.}
$$
